const description = `Dishes coming out wet? Same-day LA. Why Bosch condensation dryers need rinse aid, when it's the heating element, and the 8 real causes. BHGS #A49573. $89 dx waived.`;

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why does my Bosch dishwasher leave dishes wet when it never used to?",
    a: "Most Bosch (and Miele) dishwashers dry by condensation, not a heating element — they rely on a hot final rinse plus a cool stainless tub so moisture condenses on the tub wall and drains away. The single biggest reason a condensation dryer starts leaving dishes wet is an empty rinse-aid dispenser: rinse aid breaks the water's surface tension so it sheets off instead of clinging in droplets. Refill it first. If it's a CrystalDry or AutoAir model and drying still degrades with rinse aid topped up, the zeolite/fan path or the auto-door-open actuator may be failing, which is a technician diagnosis. Bosch also notes the machine must be installed level for the auto-open door to work. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "My plastic containers are always wet but the glasses are dry. Is the dishwasher broken?",
    a: "No — that's physics, not a fault, especially on condensation dryers (Bosch, Miele). Glass and ceramic hold heat, so residual warmth evaporates the last droplets; plastic holds almost no heat, so water just sits on it. Bosch documents this directly. The fixes are habit, not repair: keep rinse aid full, choose an Extra Dry or Sanitize option if you have one, crack the door open for a few minutes at the end, and give plastic items a quick shake. If glass and ceramic are ALSO coming out soaked, then it's worth a service look."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I know if it's the heating element or just rinse aid?",
    a: "First, which kind of dryer do you have? If it's a Bosch or Miele (condensation drying, no exposed element), there's no heating element to fail — chase rinse aid, loading, and the dry option. If it's a US-style heated-dry machine (many GE, Whirlpool, Frigidaire, Maytag, KitchenAid — you'll see a metal calrod element on the tub floor), then a burned-out element or a tripped high-limit thermostat is a real possibility, and dishes usually come out wet AND the water feels under-heated. On Whirlpool-family machines that fault often shows as the F7E1 code (heating element doesn't work). We test the element and thermostat with a meter on-site. Element or thermostat replacement runs $180 to $460."
  },
  {
    q: "The rinse-aid dispenser is full but dishes are still wet. Now what?",
    a: "A full dispenser that isn't actually releasing rinse aid mimics an empty one. Check that the dispenser cap is seated and not cracked (GE and Bosch both call this out), and that the setting isn't turned to the lowest dose. If the cap and dose are fine and dishes stay wet, the next suspects depend on your machine: on a heated-dry model, the element or high-limit thermostat; on a condensation model, the final-rinse water temperature (a weak heater means a cool rinse and poor condensation) or a fan/vent fault on fan-assisted units. That's where an on-site test earns its keep."
  },
  {
    q: "Does the way I load and unload really change drying?",
    a: "More than people expect. Unload the BOTTOM rack first — both Bosch and GE recommend this — so water pooled on upper items doesn't drip down onto dishes that already dried. Don't overcrowd; leave space for air and heat to move. Turn cups and concave items so they don't cradle a puddle, and shake off dimpled or etched pieces where water collects. On condensation dryers especially, loading discipline is half the drying result."
  },
  {
    q: "Could it be that I'm not selecting a drying option?",
    a: "Very often, yes. Many 'not drying' complaints are simply an eco cycle or a dry option left off. Bosch's Extra Dry and Sanitize options add heat to the final rinse; GE machines have Heated Dry, Power Dry, Dry Boost, or Hi-Temp Rinse. Heavier wash cycles also run hotter and dry better than quick or eco cycles. If you've been running the shortest cycle to save time, that's likely your answer — pick a hotter cycle or turn on the dry boost and test a load before booking a visit."
  },
  {
    q: "There's white film and spots — is the dishwasher not drying?",
    a: "That's usually hard-water mineral spotting, not a drying fault — Southern California water is hard, and the deposits get mistaken for water that didn't dry. Rinse aid helps a lot (it's what makes water sheet off), and units with a built-in softener use dishwasher salt. Also check water temperature: GE recommends incoming water at 120 to 140°F, so run the hot tap at the sink right before starting a load. If the spots wipe off as powder, it's minerals; if it's actual droplets, it's drying. Different problems, different fixes."
  },
  {
    q: "When is 'not drying' actually a repair versus a habit fix?",
    a: "Habit fixes cover most calls: refill rinse aid, pick a hotter or extra-dry cycle, load smarter, unload bottom-first, crack the door at the end. It becomes a real repair when: a heated-dry machine has a dead element or high-limit thermostat (dishes wet plus cool water), a condensation model's heater can't reach final-rinse temperature, a rinse-aid dispenser is physically broken, or a CrystalDry/AutoAir/fan mechanism has failed. Our $89 diagnostic tells you which side of that line you're on, and it's waived if you go ahead with the repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What's your warranty?",
    a: "90 days on SDAR labor and parts for the work we perform. Same component fails inside 90 days, we replace it free. BHGS #A49573, BBB Accredited Business, EPA 608 Universal certified #1346255700410. $89 residential diagnostic, waived when you approve the repair."
  }
];

