<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>The first question isn't "what broke" — it's "how does your dishwasher dry?"</h2>

    <p><strong>Two designs dry two different ways, and the fix depends on which you own. <span class="hl">Condensation dryers</span> (Bosch, Miele, most modern units) have no heating element — they use a hot final rinse and a cool stainless tub so moisture condenses and drains. They live or die on rinse aid, and they dry plastic poorly by design. <span class="hl">Heated-dry machines</span> (many GE, Whirlpool, Frigidaire, Maytag, KitchenAid) have an exposed metal element or a vent fan that actively evaporates water, so a dead element or tripped thermostat leaves dishes wet. The single most common quick fix on either type: refill the rinse-aid dispenser. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Here's the honest version most search results skip: the majority of "not drying" calls aren't broken parts at all. They're an empty rinse-aid dispenser, an eco cycle with no heat, a machine packed too tight, or plastic that was never going to dry on a condensation unit. Our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair work those out first, because there's no reason to sell you a part when the fix is a $6 bottle of rinse aid.</p>

    <p>Below we walk the design split, then the eight real causes in honest order. Parent pillar: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/">dishwasher repair</a>. Related: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/not-cleaning/">dishwasher not cleaning</a> · <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/error-codes/">dishwasher error codes</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">The design split</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Condensation drying vs heated dry — know which you have.</h2>
    <p class="lead">This one distinction explains most drying complaints. Open the door and look at the tub floor: a thick metal loop (a calrod element) means heated dry; a smooth floor with no visible element means condensation.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Condensation drying</th><th>Heated dry / fan</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Brands</td><td>Bosch, Miele, most modern EU-style</td><td>Many GE, Whirlpool, Frigidaire, Maytag, KitchenAid</td></tr>
        <tr><td>How it dries</td><td>Hot final rinse + cool stainless tub; moisture condenses on the wall and drains</td><td>Exposed element or vent fan evaporates water</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rinse aid</td><td>Essentially required</td><td>Strongly recommended</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plastic</td><td>Dries poorly (holds no heat)</td><td>Dries better (element adds heat)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>What fails</td><td>Weak final-rinse heat, fan/zeolite path, auto-open actuator</td><td>Burned-out element, tripped high-limit thermostat</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>So a Bosch owner and a Whirlpool owner with wet dishes have genuinely different problems. Bosch documents that its condensation process runs "instead of utilizing a heating element" and that plastics "do not retain heat"; GE documents its heating element as "a thin circular tube at the bottom of the tub." Match the fix to the machine.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 1</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rinse aid is empty — the number-one cause on both designs.</h2>
    <p>Rinse aid lowers water's surface tension so it <em>sheets</em> off the dishes instead of beading into droplets that cling and leave the load wet (and spotty). GE and Bosch both document this directly, and it's the first thing we check on every drying call. On a condensation dryer it's not optional — without rinse aid, those machines simply don't dry well. Refill the dispenser (the cap on the inside of the door), set it to a mid or high dose, and run a load. This alone resolves a large share of "not drying" complaints. If your dispenser is full but still not helping, jump to cause 5 (a broken dispenser mimics an empty one).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 2</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Bosch CrystalDry, AutoAir, and PureDry — and what fails.</h2>
    <p>Bosch's higher lines add drying tech on top of condensation. <strong>PureDry</strong> is the standard closed condensation process. <strong>CrystalDry</strong> uses a natural mineral, zeolite, that adsorbs humidity and releases heat (Bosch cites up to ~176°F), recirculating hot dry air — Bosch claims markedly better drying, including plastics. <strong>AutoAir</strong> motorizes the door to pop open at cycle end and vent the moist air.</p>
    <p>When drying degrades on these models with rinse aid full: AutoAir won't open correctly if the machine isn't installed level (Bosch explicitly cites leveling), or its door actuator has failed; CrystalDry leans on its sealed zeolite chamber, fan, and heater path, so a fan or heater fault there is a technician diagnosis. These aren't customer-reset items — if a leveled machine with rinse aid still won't dry, book a look.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 3</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Heating element or high-limit thermostat (heated-dry models only).</h2>
    <p>On calrod heated-dry machines — many GE, Whirlpool, Frigidaire, Maytag, KitchenAid — a burned-out heating element or a tripped/failed high-limit thermostat leaves dishes wet, and usually the wash water feels under-heated too. On Whirlpool-family units this often surfaces as the <span class="code">F7E1</span> code ("heating element doesn't work"), which is OEM-published. Our techs meter the element and thermostat on-site; replacement runs $180 to $460. This cause does <strong>not</strong> apply to pure-condensation Bosch or Miele machines — they have no element to fail, which is exactly why the design split matters.</p>
    <p>See also: <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-error-codes/">Whirlpool dishwasher error codes</a> (the F#E# heat codes) and <a href="/services/dishwasher-repair/whirlpool-not-drying/">Whirlpool dishwasher not drying</a> (brand-specific).</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 4</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The dry option was never turned on.</h2>
    <p>A big chunk of "not drying" is simply an eco or quick cycle running with no heat. Bosch's Extra Dry and Sanitize options add heat to the final rinse; GE machines have Heated Dry, Power Dry, Dry Boost, or Hi-Temp Rinse. Heavier wash cycles run hotter and dry better than the short ones. If you've been choosing the fastest cycle, switch to a hotter one or enable the dry boost and test a load — that's free, and it's often the whole answer.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 5</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Rinse-aid dispenser failure — a full one that isn't dispensing.</h2>
    <p>Distinct from an empty dispenser: the cap can crack or unseat, or the dispenser can stop releasing rinse aid even when full. GE tells owners to confirm the dispenser cap is seated and functioning; Bosch addresses the dispenser as a serviceable component. If your dispenser is topped up and dishes still come out wet, a failed dispenser is a real suspect — and it's an inexpensive fix relative to an element or control repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 6</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Loading, plastic, and unloading order.</h2>
    <p>Loading discipline is half the drying result, especially on condensation dryers. Both Bosch and GE recommend unloading the <strong>bottom rack first</strong>, so water pooled on upper items doesn't rain down on dishes that already dried. Don't overcrowd — leave space for air and heat to move. Turn cups and bowls so they don't cradle a puddle, and give dimpled or etched items a shake (GE notes water pools in "dimples, indents or etching"). And accept the physics: plastic holds almost no heat, so on a condensation machine it will be the last thing to dry, if it dries at all.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Cause 7</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Hard-water spotting mistaken for wet dishes.</h2>
    <p>Southern California water is hard, and the white film or spots it leaves get confused with water that didn't dry. If it wipes off as a powder, it's minerals, not moisture — rinse aid (which sheets water off) is the main fix, and units with a built-in softener use dishwasher salt. Water temperature matters too: GE recommends incoming water at 120 to 140°F, so run the hot tap at the sink right before you start a load. Cold fill water both under-dries and worsens spotting.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY checklist</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Run through these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Refill rinse aid</strong> and set a mid/high dose. Number-one fix on both designs.</li>
      <li><strong>Pick a hotter cycle</strong> or turn on Extra Dry / Dry Boost / Heated Dry.</li>
      <li><strong>Run the hot tap</strong> at the sink first so the fill water is 120–140°F.</li>
      <li><strong>Load with space</strong>; angle cups and bowls so they don't hold water.</li>
      <li><strong>Unload the bottom rack first</strong> to avoid drip-down.</li>
      <li><strong>Crack the door</strong> open a few minutes at the end (helps condensation dryers a lot).</li>
      <li><strong>Accept plastic</strong> won't fully dry on a condensation machine — shake it off.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If glass and ceramic are still wet after all that, or the wash water feels cool on a heated-dry unit, that's when it's a real repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Most "not drying" is a habit fix. Here's the line.</h2>
    <p>We'd rather save you a service call than sell you one. Refill rinse aid, choose a hotter cycle, load smarter, unload bottom-first, crack the door — that clears the majority of drying complaints for free. It becomes a genuine repair when a heated-dry machine has a dead element or high-limit thermostat (wet dishes plus cool water), a condensation model can't reach final-rinse temperature, a rinse-aid dispenser is physically broken, or a CrystalDry/AutoAir/fan mechanism has failed. The $89 diagnostic tells you exactly which side of that line you're on, and it's waived if you approve the repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Drying-repair costs in Southern California.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
